<!-- What is painted before Compose exists. The in-app palette lives in :core:designsystem. -->
<resources>
    <!--
      The near-neutral black the dark theme uses for `surface`, restated here
      because a launcher composites the adaptive icon before any Compose theme
      exists, so this layer cannot read a token. It is the same value as the
      dark `surface` in :core:designsystem, and the two move together — it was
      a warm black until that ground was re-derived to stop reading brown.

      The icon artwork itself is a stand-in. The palette, the app icon and the
      wider icon set are open decision OD-071, and nothing here invents a brand
      colour ahead of it.
    -->
    <color name="ic_launcher_background">#FF0E0D0C</color>
    <!--
      The two grounds, restated for the same reason: they are painted before
      Compose exists — by the platform, and by the activity in onCreate — and
      neither painter can read a token. Light is the paper `surface`, dark the
      near-neutral black one, and both move with :core:designsystem.

      Unqualified on purpose. Which ground the window stands on is a question
      about the Appearance setting, which screen SCR-407 allows to disagree
      with the device, so the browser Activity picks between these two by the theme it
      resolved rather than letting the night qualifier answer for it.
    -->
    <color name="window_ground_light">#FFFBF8F3</color>
    <color name="window_ground_dark">#FF0E0D0C</color>

    <!--
      What is painted before this app is running to be asked: the system
      splash, and the window until the browser Activity levels it. This one alone is
      night-qualified, because the device is the only thing the platform has
      to go on at that point — nothing has read the stored Appearance yet, and
      on a cold start nothing has reached the disk it is on.
    -->
    <color name="splash_background">@color/window_ground_light</color>
</resources>
